 The Nyquist rate is the minimum sampling rate required to avoid aliasing, given by the Nyquist frequency multiplied by two. The Nyquist frequency is half the sampling rate. For a signal Sa(200t), the Nyquist rate is 200/(2π) cps, which simplifies to 100/π cps. The Nyquist interval is the reciprocal of the Nyquist rate, so it's 2π/100 cps, which is π/50 sec. For the squared signal [Sa(200t)]^2, the Nyquist rate is twice that of the original signal, so it's 200/π cps, and the Nyquist interval is π/100 sec. Looking at the options, option B matches these calculations.
