After constructing the truth table, we'll compare the columns for (I ≡ ~H) and ~(~I ≡ H). If they have the same truth values in all rows, they are logically equivalent. If they have opposite truth values in all rows, they are contradictory. If they have some rows where they are the same and some where they are different, they are neither equivalent nor contradictory.

Finally, we'll determine if they are consistent or inconsistent based on whether there's at least one row where both propositions are true or both are false. If there's no such row, they are inconsistent. If there is at least one row where they are both true or both false, they are consistent.

After analyzing the truth table, we'll conclude whether the statements are logically equivalent, contradictory, consistent, or inconsistent.
