 The magnetic force on a moving charge is given by F = q(v × B). Since the proton is moving perpendicular to the magnetic field, the force is F = qvB. This force provides the centripetal force required for circular motion, so F = mv²/r. Setting these equal, qvB = mv²/r, which simplifies to v = (qBr)/(m). 

Given that the charge of a proton is 1.6 × 10^-19 C, the magnetic field is 1.0 Wb/m², and the radius of curvature is 0.22 m, we can plug these values into the equation. 

v = (1.6 × 10^-19 C × 1.0 Wb/m² × 0.22 m) / (1.67 × 10^-27 kg) 

Calculating the numerator: 1.6 × 10^-19 × 0.22 = 3.52 × 10^-20 

Dividing by the mass of the proton: 3.52 × 10^-20 / 1.67 × 10^-27 ≈ 2.1 × 10^7 m/s 

So the speed of the proton is approximately 2.1 × 10^7 m/s. Looking at the options, option I is 2.1 × 10^7 m/s, which matches our calculation.
