The number of collisions per second and the time between collisions can be calculated using the given values. 

**Number of collisions per second:**
\[
\text{Number of collisions per second} = \frac{\text{Electron speed}}{\text{Distance between collisions}} = \frac{1.21 \times 10^6 \, \text{m/s}}{4 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{m}} = 3.025 \times 10^{13} \, \text{collisions/s}
\]

**Time between collisions:**
\[
\text{Time between collisions} = \frac{\text{Distance between collisions}}{\text{Electron speed}} = \frac{4 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{m}}{1.21 \times 10^6 \, \text{m/s}} \approx 3.3 \times 10^{-14} \, \text{seconds}
\]

**Answer:** H. 3.0 × 10^13 collisions/s and 3.3 × 10^-14 s
